window.CHALK_TEXT = {
 "00-chalk-book-guide": "00 · Meta Chalk Book Guide Purpose, structure & conventions A personal technical chalk book. After a session of problem-solving, building or learning with an AI, the work is distilled into one calm, skimmable note that can be revisited later for a fast refresher, on a small local site or as a PDF. This is the read-only copy of CLAUDE.md , which is the source of truth. Local — your machine Remote — the server Config file Concept AWS Console Gotcha Best practice Paths Purpose Capture recent work while it is fresh: commands, configs, decisions, mistakes. Revisit it later and get the gist in under a minute. Repeat it cold, months later, without re-researching anything. Trace what was done, per project and date, so a future AI can recall which scenarios have been worked through. Two kinds of knowledge Recurring Used again and again: systemctl , ssh config, naming rules. The note is a fast reference. Patterns, quick tables, the one flag you always forget. One-off Set up once, or a rare fix: growing an EBS volume, a logrotate permission error. The note is a complete recipe. Every step in order, every flag explained, so it can be redone from zero. Best practice · When in doubt, write for the forgetful future self. Assume nothing is remembered. The collection chalk-book/ ├── README.md # what this is; points here ├── CLAUDE.md # this guide (source of truth) ├── chalk-pastel.css # the note colour palette (user-owned) ├── index.html # the site: open it in a browser ├── site/ # catalogue, menu, search (build-index.mjs), dark theme ├── notes/ # one HTML file per note │ ├── tinker-town-01-aws-ssh-firewall.html … │ ├── note-layout.css # shared layout and components │ └── fonts/ # bundled Inter, renders offline ├── archive/ # old PDFs, including the last guide PDF │ └── backlog-workflow/ # the old backlog design (private) ├── .prog/, .research/ # private and gitignored: backlog, research └── .claude/skills/memory-dump/ # the note-making workflow File naming <project>-<NN>-<topic>.html Part Rule Example project Slug from the project list, so the name says where the work happened. tinker-town NN Two digits, counting up within each project. 03 topic 2–4 words naming the subject, not the outcome. cron-logrotate scope One topic per file. A long session becomes several notes. — date Inside the note and in site/notes.js , not in the file name. 2026-08-30 00- Reserved for meta files about the chalk book itself. 00-chalk-book-guide Projects Slug Display name What it is tinker-town Project Tinker Town Self-paced AWS Linux sysadmin lab, a one-week crash course in 7 modules pokedex Pokédex The Pokédex web project meta Chalk Book Notes about the chalk book itself A new project gets a row here and in CHALK_PROJECTS in site/notes.js , which can also hold its roadmap of modules. Note types Walkthrough What was done in a session, start to finish. Most notes are this type. Reference A cross-cutting rulebook other notes depend on, such as naming conventions. It says so in its summary. Anatomy of a note Every note follows the same top-to-bottom shape, so the eye always knows where to look. Header band : eyebrow chip PROJECT TINKER TOWN · 03 · MODULE 1 · 2026-08-30 , title words coloured blue, pink, green, yellow in turn, a short subtitle, and a 1–2 sentence past-tense summary. Legend : colour chips for every block type used in the note. What we covered : a ✓ checklist of outcomes, skimmable on its own. Full command sequence : steps under short headings, each tagged Local or Remote Supporting sections : concepts, diagrams, field tables, quick-reference tables. Bugs hit & fixed : one card per real error, Cause → Fix . Where things live and Best practices (short cards) as needed. Part of line: the project, with links to related notes through the site. Footer on printed pages: Tinker Town 03 — Systemd Service · 1 / 3 Block types Block Palette colour Used for Local --sky Commands run on your own machine Remote --lav Commands run on the server Config file --butter File contents, with the path as the block title Concept --peri The why behind something; command output Gotcha --peach Traps that bit, or nearly did; bug cards Best practice --mint The recommended way, and why Paths --pink File and folder locations; tables; title accent AWS Console --coral Steps done in a web console, not a shell Inline callouts use a bold label and a dot: Gotcha · Concept · Key rule · Naming note · Updated rule · Writing style Skim first. Headings, checklists and tables carry the story; prose only fills gaps. Short. Sentences under ~20 words. No intro paragraphs, no filler. Every command gets a comment explaining what it does and what each flag means. Config files stay clean. Annotations sit beside the file or in a field table, never inside it. Real by default, except access, location and private life. Actual commands, paths and error messages, but: no keys, passwords or tokens; placeholders such as <instance-ip> for public IPs, account IDs and emails; nothing about the user's private life. Anything that looks like an admin account is always adm-kiwi : never a name, never a placeholder, live or test. Keep the mistakes. Errors and their fixes are often the most valuable part. Respect history. When a convention changes, mark old names as legacy instead of rewriting earlier notes. Calm tone. Plain, friendly and factual. No hype, no emoji. sudo mkdir -p /mnt/data # -p: create parents, no error if it exists Gotcha · Many config formats break on inline comments. That is why config blocks are never annotated inside the block itself. Visual style Key rule · chalk-pastel.css is the palette for every note, until the user says otherwise. Colours are used only through its variables, never as hex values. The file belongs to the user. How the palette is built Part Example Used for chalk --lav Chips, borders, left bars -bg --lav-bg Box tints -ink --lav-ink Coloured text, comments -chip --lav-chip Text on a chalk chip Concept · Notes link the palette instead of copying it. Changing chalk-pastel.css restyles every note at its next render; PDFs keep their look until re-rendered. Layout lives in notes/note-layout.css : A4 on --paper , an --asphalt header band, Inter (bundled) for text, DejaVu Sans Mono (from the system, falls back to any monospace) for code, soft tinted blocks. The site index.html opens straight from disk, no build step. How it looks and behaves is in the code and in meta-01-site-build . Site colours live in site/site-theme.css , never in the note palette. The name is always the shared <chalk-wordmark> . After adding or changing a note, rebuild the search index: node site/build-index.mjs . A new site page goes in site/pages/ , then into PAGES in site/app.js and the main footer. New Export options are items in the Export menu. Backlog and Complete Tasks are private pages in .prog/ , gitignored, about Chalk Book itself only. A finished item moves to Complete Tasks only when the user asks, unchanged except a Done chip and a date. How notes are made & changed Concept · The notes are read-only by design . Nobody edits a note in a viewer; every change goes through an AI session. Adding a note: the memory-dump skill Work on something, in any project folder. While it is fresh, say “memory-dump” or “chalk that up” . A personal pointer skill makes this work in every folder. An AI may also offer a dump when the topic changes or about an hour of content has built up. The skill plans the notes, fills the template in notes/ , adds them to site/notes.js , rebuilds the search index, runs check.sh and checks every page, then hands over following the user's own git preferences. Check the note on the site and ask for any changes in the same session. Changing a note or this guide Ask for the change in a session. The AI edits the HTML source or CLAUDE.md . Note PDFs are made on demand from the site; the originals from before the HTML rebuild and the last guide PDF are kept in archive/ . Local Rendering and checks .claude/skills/memory-dump/render.sh notes/<name>.html [out-dir] # → <name>.pdf .claude/skills/memory-dump/check.sh [--render] # must pass before every commit Rules for AI assistants Read CLAUDE.md before creating or changing anything in the chalk book. Use the memory-dump skill to make notes, and chalk-pastel.css for every colour. Match the existing notes' structure and look exactly; open one as a reference if unsure. Never edit, rename or delete an existing note or archived PDF unless the user asks. Keep CLAUDE.md and this guide's HTML in sync, and change them only when the user asks. Keep private data out before it is committed: GitHub may still serve a pushed commit after a force-push. If something slips through, tell the user; don't rewrite history without asking. Git follows the person. Committing, pushing and commit-message style come from the current user's own instructions, never from this guide. If none are given, ask before any git action. Git wording. Use git's own terms exactly: stage , commit , push , pull , revert , reset . If a request doesn't map clearly to one git action, ask, offering 1–3 options with a short description each. Commit messages document the work: a title, then a body saying what changed and why.",
 "meta-01-site-build": "Chalk Book · 01 · 2026-10-12 Site & Workflow Build From a folder of PDFs to a browsable, searchable notebook Renamed log-book to Chalk Book. Set up a memory-dump skill that works from any folder. Rebuilt the notes as pastel HTML and built a dark static site to browse, search and export them. Pushed it all to a private GitHub repo. Every later note comes out of this workflow. The rules live in CLAUDE.md , and the guide is a copy of them. Local — your machine Config file Concept Gotcha Best practice Paths What we covered Renamed everything from log-book to chalk-book A personal pointer skill so \"memory dump\" and \"chalk that up\" work in every project The AI offers a dump when the topic changes or about 60 minutes of content has built up The file naming standard <project>-<NN>-<topic> , and the projects list (Project Tinker Town) Rebuilt the original PDFs as HTML notes on chalk-pastel.css , and kept the originals in archive/ A static site in Claude dark-mode greys, with no build step, that opens straight from disk Full-text search with highlighting, a Recently viewed rail, and Export → Save as PDF A persistent main footer with Contact, plus private Backlog and Complete Tasks pages in .prog/ Pushed to the private GitHub repo alphanatic/chalk-book over HTTPS, with private folders kept out The workflow Work in any project → \"chalk that up\" → memory-dump skill → note in notes/ → site + push Piece Where Job Pointer skill ~/.claude/skills/memory-dump/ Recognises the trigger words in any folder and points back to the chalk book Real skill .claude/skills/memory-dump/ The procedure: plan, template, catalogue, index, render check, commit Prompting rule ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md Offer a dump on a topic change or about 60 minutes of content, once per trigger Rules CLAUDE.md The source of truth, mirrored in the guide's HTML and PDF Concept · Claude Code only loads a project's CLAUDE.md and skills when it starts in that folder. A small skill in ~/.claude/skills/ is visible everywhere, so it acts as a signpost back to the project. Full command sequence Local Add a note and refresh search n=meta-01-site-build # the new note's name cp .claude/skills/memory-dump/template.html notes/$n.html # start from the skeleton node site/build-index.mjs # rebuild site/search-index.js .claude/skills/memory-dump/render.sh notes/$n.html /tmp # A4 PDF via headless Brave pdftoppm -r 70 -png /tmp/$n.pdf /tmp/p # one PNG per page Local First push over HTTPS (no SSH key on this machine) git init -b main # new repo, branch main git config user.email \"<github-noreply-or-outlook>\" # never a home address git remote add origin https://github.com/alphanatic/chalk-book.git gh auth setup-git # gh acts as git's credential helper git add -A && git status --short # check nothing private is staged git commit -m \"Chalk Book: original version\" git push -u origin main # -u: track origin/main Config Private folders kept out of git .gitignore .research/ market and competitor notes .prog/ the Backlog and Complete Tasks pages .claude/settings.local.json personal permission rules archive/backlog-workflow/ records of the old backlog design .vscode/ editor settings How the site works index.html top bar, left menu, viewer, Recently viewed, main footer site/notes.js catalogue: projects, roadmaps, notes search-index.js plain text of every note, generated notes/*.html one A4 sheet each, shown in an iframe Gotcha · When the site is opened from disk ( file:// ), every file counts as its own origin. The site can't fetch data or reach into the note's iframe. So the data is loaded as <script> files, and the site talks to the note with postMessage . Concept Messages between the site and a note Site sends Note ( notes/note.js ) does \"chalk:print\" Opens the print dialog: Export → Save as PDF {type: \"chalk:highlight\", q} Wraps every hit in mark.chalk-hit , even across elements, then scrolls to the first Routes Site pages by address site/app.js const PAGES = { backlog: { title: \"Backlog\", file: \".prog/index.html\", hideRecent: true }, complete: { title: \"Complete Tasks\", file: \".prog/complete.html\" }, contact: { title: \"Contact\", file: \"site/pages/contact.html\" }, }; index.html#contact opens a page. Any other hash is treated as a note id. To add a page, put the file in site/pages/ , then add it to PAGES and to the footer. Behaviour Panels and header Part Behaviour Left menu nav#nav-menu Starts as a rail (number + colour bar). Only the menu button opens and closes it Recently viewed A rail on the right. Hover slides it open over the note in 0.3 s; leaving slides it back. Last 10, kept in localStorage Search Full text, phrase first. Each note is listed once with its hit count; opening it highlights every hit <chalk-wordmark> One custom element for the name: Chalk in blue, Book in yellow Note titles note.js colours the words blue → pink → green → yellow, with every & in lavender Narrow windows The name is hidden first (820 px), then the note title (600 px) Private Backlog and Complete Tasks Both live in .prog/ . They're gitignored, so online the footer links lead to empty pages. They cover Chalk Book itself only. Work inside a project goes in that project's roadmap in site/notes.js . An item moves to Complete Tasks only when asked : its whole card, with a Done chip and a date added. It keeps its number and id , and no details are invented. Bugs hit & fixed Bug · note sheet showed a beige frame instead of dark CAUSE With no background on html , the browser spreads the body background (paper) over the whole canvas of the iframe. FIX Give html a transparent \"image\" background, and set color-scheme: dark to match the site (screen only). notes/note-layout.css :root { color-scheme: dark; } html { background: linear-gradient(transparent, transparent); } Bug · hidden iframe still showing CAUSE A display: block rule beat the browser's built-in [hidden] style. FIX [hidden] { display: none !important } Bug · searching \"module 2\" matched every note CAUSE The words were matched one at a time, and \"module\" and \"2\" appear almost everywhere. FIX Match the whole phrase first, and fall back to separate words only when the phrase isn't found. Bug · highlighted header text unreadable CAUSE Light title text on a peach highlight. FIX Mark text uses var(--ink) on screen. In print, highlights are hidden and the text keeps its own colour ( color: inherit ). Bug · Backlog opened halfway down the page CAUSE The route pointed to an anchor ( #naming-parts ) inside the page. FIX Route to the bare file, .prog/index.html . Bug · headless test browser kept the profile locked CAUSE Piping a test into | head killed the script with EPIPE, and the headless Brave it started kept running. FIX Find and stop it, then don't pipe test runners into head . pgrep -af remote-debugging-port # -a: full command line, -f: match against it kill <pid> # only a process you started yourself Bug · a public IP inside an archived PDF CAUSE An original PDF showed a server's public IP, which shouldn't be in the repo. FIX Redacted it with PyMuPDF in a scratch venv, replacing it with <instance-ip> , then amended the only commit and force-pushed. for page in doc: # doc = fitz.open(\"archive/….pdf\") for r in page.search_for(ip): # every spot the text appears page.add_redact_annot(r, text=\"<instance-ip>\") page.apply_redactions() # deletes the text, not just hides it Gotcha · A force-push rewrites the branch, but GitHub can still serve the old commit to anyone who has its exact link. Keep private data out before the first push. Where things live What Path Rules (source of truth) CLAUDE.md → notes/00-chalk-book-guide.html → 00-chalk-book-guide.pdf Note palette (user-owned) chalk-pastel.css Dark site greys site/site-theme.css Shared note layout and script notes/note-layout.css , notes/note.js Site logic and styles site/app.js , site/app.css Contact page site/pages/contact.html Private progress pages .prog/index.html , .prog/complete.html Original PDFs archive/ Best practices Two palettes, kept apart Notes use only chalk-pastel.css , and the site frame uses only site-theme.css . No hex values anywhere else. No build step Plain files that open from disk and can be hosted anywhere unchanged. Generated data goes into script files. Test headless Drive headless Brave over CDP from small node scripts to check layout, search and highlighting. Access ≠ permission Setting up push access is not a request to push. Push, publish or force-push only when asked, every time. Private stays local Backlog, research and records go in gitignored folders. Check git status before every commit. Rules in one place Change CLAUDE.md only on request, then update the guide HTML and PDF too. Part of Chalk Book · see 00 Chalk Book Guide for the full rules",
 "meta-02-privacy-checks": "Chalk Book · 02 · 2026-10-12 Privacy Rule & Check Script Rules that a script enforces, not memory Wrote a privacy rule into CLAUDE.md and masked every admin account as adm-kiwi , in notes and archived PDFs alike. Built check.sh , which every commit must pass. Backlog items 1 and 2 are done. Every later note is checked by the same script before it is committed. Local — your machine Config file Concept Gotcha Best practice Paths What we covered A privacy rule in CLAUDE.md §4: real by default, except access, location and private life Every admin account written as adm-kiwi , live or test, with no placeholder and no explanation Swapped text inside archived PDFs with PyMuPDF, in each PDF's own font check.sh : one command for placeholders, colours, secrets, IPs, emails, admin names and the catalogue An optional private block list outside the repo, for words no general check can recognise Workflow rules: commit locally, push only when asked, ask before marking a backlog item done Backlog: item 25 added (legends that match the page), items 1 and 2 marked done The privacy rule Kind Rule Example Access Never written, not even partly masked keys, passwords, tokens, recovery codes Location Becomes a <what-it-is> placeholder <instance-ip> , <my-ip> , <vpc-id> Private life Left out; when in doubt, ask real name, home address, job search, health Admin accounts Always adm-kiwi , never a name or a pattern ssh adm-kiwi@<instance-ip> Concept · Private ranges ( 10.0.0.0/16 ), 0.0.0.0/0 and role accounts such as svc-disk-report are fine. They don't identify a person or a host on the internet. Gotcha · A pushed commit can't be fully taken back. GitHub may still serve it by its hash after a force-push. Keep private data out before the first commit. Full command sequence Local Run the checks .claude/skills/memory-dump/check.sh # every check; exit 1 if any problem .claude/skills/memory-dump/check.sh --render # also renders changed notes, prints page counts 2. Placeholders notes/tinker-town-03-systemd-service.html:16: …{{x}}… leftover {{…}} placeholder 8. Catalogue site/notes.js: tinker-town-04-cron-logrotate has n: 7, file says 04 catalogue ... ✗ 55 problem(s) Output from the test copy with planted problems. The real repo prints ✓ all checks passed . Concept What check.sh checks # Check Scans 1 Rebuilds the search index — 2 No {{…}} placeholders notes 3 No hard-coded hex colours notes 4 No private keys, AKIA… keys, ghp_… tokens all committed text and PDFs 5 No public IPs, 12-digit account IDs or emails (allowed: git@github.com ) all committed text and PDFs 6 Admin accounts are only adm-kiwi : catches adm-… , admin-… , …_admin all committed text and PDFs 7 No word from the private block list everything, incl. the search index 8 Every note has a catalogue entry and the reverse; ids unique; n matches the file notes/ , site/notes.js Config Private block list (outside the repo) ~/.config/chalk-book/blocklist # one word per line lines starting with # are skipped <real-username> matched as a whole word, any case <surname> fill in yourself; never in a transcript Missing file: check 7 is skipped. It lives outside the repo, so it can never be committed. Local Swap text inside a PDF (one-off recipe) python3 -m venv ~/venv-pdf && ~/venv-pdf/bin/pip install pymupdf fonttools brotli ~/venv-pdf/bin/python -c \"from fontTools.ttLib import TTFont # woff2 → ttf for PyMuPDF f = TTFont('inter.woff2'); f.flavor = None; f.save('Inter.ttf')\" for page in doc: # doc = pymupdf.open(path) for r in page.search_for(old): # every spot the text appears page.add_redact_annot(r, fill=bg) # bg: colour sampled beside the text page.apply_redactions(images=0, graphics=0) # remove the text, keep chips and lines page.insert_text((r.x0, span_baseline), new, fontsize=span_size, fontfile=\"DejaVuSansMono.ttf\", color=span_colour) # same font, size, colour doc.save(tmp, garbage=4, deflate=True) # garbage=4: drop the old text objects diff <(pdftotext -layout before.pdf -) <(pdftotext -layout after.pdf -) # only the swap differs Workflow rules Commit, don't push The AI commits once check.sh passes. Pushing waits for an explicit \"push it\". Ask before done The AI says when a backlog item is finished and waits for a yes before marking it. Suggest, then do Backlog items start as a written suggestion. Work starts on \"do it\". Rule once, check always A rule lives in CLAUDE.md once; the skill points to it and check.sh enforces it. Bugs hit & fixed Bug · check.sh printed problems but said \"all checks passed\" CAUSE grep … | report runs report in a subshell. Its problems=$((problems+1)) was lost when the pipe ended. FIX Count failures in a file instead, and read the count at the end. fail() { echo \" $1\"; echo x >> \"$tmp/problems\"; } # survives the subshell problems=0; [ -f \"$tmp/problems\" ] && problems=$(wc -l < \"$tmp/problems\") Bug · clean run exited 1 with no summary CAUSE cat missing-file | wc -l fails, and set -euo pipefail stops the script there. FIX Test that the file exists before counting (the line above). Bug · swapped PDF text came out tiny, on grey boxes CAUSE The redaction's replacement text used a built-in PDF font at a fitted size, and the default fill was grey. FIX Restore the backups, redact with the sampled background colour, then draw the new text with insert_text in the original font file, size and colour. Bug · check.sh flagged CLAUDE.md itself CAUSE The rule's own wording showed the forbidden pattern as an example. FIX Reworded it to \"never a placeholder\", so the repo never shows the pattern at all. Bug · zsh: ==REAL not found CAUSE In zsh, a word starting with = expands to a command's path, so echo ===REAL fails. FIX Quote it, echo \"===REAL\" , or use another marker. Gotcha · Claude Code blocks an AI from editing session transcripts. To scrub a word from ~/.claude/projects/ , close every session and run the replacement yourself in a terminal. Where things live What Path The rules (privacy in §4, AI rules in §7) CLAUDE.md The check script .claude/skills/memory-dump/check.sh Private block list ~/.config/chalk-book/blocklist Claude Code transcripts (deleted after 30 days by default) ~/.claude/projects/<project-path>/*.jsonl Keep transcripts longer \"cleanupPeriodDays\": 365 in ~/.claude/settings.json Backlog and Complete Tasks (private) .prog/index.html , .prog/complete.html Open decisions Item Question Suggested 3 · Going online GitHub Pages (public), stay local, or a private host? Cloudflare Pages + Access: private, free, works on a phone Private pages Footer links appear only when a .prog/ page exists? A generated .prog/pages.js , from a tag in each page 25 · Legends Drop or relabel the legend in a note without blocks? Waiting Guide PDF Keep 00-chalk-book-guide.pdf , now mostly redundant? Waiting Part of Chalk Book · follows 01 Site & Workflow Build · rules in 00 Chalk Book Guide",
 "meta-03-readme-git-rules": "Chalk Book · 03 · 2026-10-13 README & Git Rules A front page for the repo, and git that follows the person Added a short README.md that points to CLAUDE.md . Rewrote the git rules so the repo no longer holds one person's habits: each user's own preferences decide committing, pushing and message style. Backlog items 4 and 6 are done. Every later session asks before each commit and uses git's exact terms. Local — your machine Config file Concept Gotcha Best practice Paths What we covered README.md : purpose, how to view, how to add a note, a link to CLAUDE.md . Restates no rule README added to the collection tree in CLAUDE.md §2, the guide HTML and the re-rendered guide PDF Three repo rules in §7: Git follows the person , Git wording , Commit messages document the work Personal git preferences moved to the global ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md : ask before each commit, never push unasked The memory-dump skill's last step became Hand over instead of \"commit, then offer to push\" An unpushed commit relabelled with git commit --amend , then three commits pushed Backlog: item 4 (README) and item 6 (lasting rules) marked done with a timestamp; a stale git step fixed Repo rules vs personal rules Where Binds Holds CLAUDE.md §7 (repo) Anyone who works on the repo Follow the user's own git preferences; if none, ask. Use git's exact terms. Messages have a title and a body ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md (global) One person, every project Ask before each commit. Never push unasked. Titles of key words only, like note titles Project memory One person, this project Points to the global file; restates nothing Concept · Personal habits in a repo file bind the next contributor too. Keep the repo neutral and let each person's own settings fill in the rest. Personal preferences come first, Claude's defaults second. Git wording Term Command What it does stage git add Picks the changes for the next commit commit git commit Saves a snapshot locally, with a message amend git commit --amend Rewrites the latest commit. Safe only before it is pushed push git push Sends local commits to GitHub pull git pull Fetches and merges commits from GitHub revert / reset git revert · git reset Undo with a new commit · move the branch back Key rule · If a request doesn't map clearly to one git action, ask. Offer 1–3 options, each with its command and one line on what it does. Full command sequence Local README and the guide node site/build-index.mjs # guide changed: rebuild the search index .claude/skills/memory-dump/render.sh \\ notes/00-chalk-book-guide.html . # re-render the guide PDF into the root pdftotext 00-chalk-book-guide.pdf - \\ | grep -n README # -: to stdout; confirm the new line is in .claude/skills/memory-dump/check.sh # every check must pass README.md # Chalk Book A personal technical chalk book: one calm, skimmable note per topic, distilled from work sessions with an AI so it can be repeated cold months later. ## View Open `index.html` in a browser. No build step, no server. Any note can be saved as PDF from the site's Export menu. ## Add a note In any Claude Code session, say \"chalk that up\" (the `memory-dump` skill). ## Rules How notes are named, written, styled and checked is all in [CLAUDE.md](CLAUDE.md). A readable copy is the guide note, `notes/00-chalk-book-guide.html`. Best practice · The README links to the rules instead of repeating them, so it can't drift. A site link and a licence line wait for backlog items 3 and 5. Local Relabel an unpushed commit, then push git log --oneline -2 # check the commit to relabel is the latest one git stash # park uncommitted changes so the amend leaves them out git commit --amend -m \"README & ...\" # --amend: replace the latest commit git stash pop # bring the parked changes back git status -sb # -s: short, -b: branch line, shows \"ahead 3\" git push # only after the user asked for it Commit messages Before After Add README.md; list it in the guide's collection tree README & Collection Tree + a body on what and why (new) Git Rules & Personal Preferences + a body on what and why Bugs hit & fixed Bug · \"Don't commit\" taken literally CAUSE \"I do not want you to commit anything any more\" was read as \"never commit\". It could also mean \"never push\" or \"ask first\". FIX Offered three options with a line each. The answer was ask before each commit . The Git wording rule now makes asking the default for unclear git requests. Bug · Personal git habits in the repo CAUSE CLAUDE.md , the skill and the guide all said \"commit locally, push only when asked\". That is one person's habit, and it bound anyone else who works on the repo. FIX The repo now only says to follow the user's own preferences. The habit itself moved to the global ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md . Bug · Message style read as design CAUSE \"Like my note titles\" was turned into Title Case, & joins and a word count. FIX It means only: key words that best capture the content. Anything unstated follows the user's preferences first, Claude's defaults second. Where things live What Path The repo's front page README.md Repo git rules CLAUDE.md §7 Personal git preferences ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md , section \"Git\" Skill hand-over step .claude/skills/memory-dump/SKILL.md step 7 Backlog and handoff (private) .prog/index.html · .prog/handoff.md Best practices Ask, don't guess An unclear git request gets 1–3 options, each with its command. Amend before pushing Rewriting a commit is safe only while it is local. Neutral repo Repo files say what anyone needs; personal habits live in personal settings. Timestamp done A Done chip carries the date and time it was marked. Part of Chalk Book · follows 02 Privacy Rule & Check Script · continued in 04 CLAUDE.md Cleanup",
 "meta-04-claude-md-cleanup": "Chalk Book · 04 · 2026-10-13 CLAUDE.md Cleanup Rules stay, description goes, and the file shrinks Audited CLAUDE.md read-only first, then cut it from 16,933 to 14,028 bytes (−17%) without losing a rule. Fixed three stale facts and synced the guide HTML. How the site looks now lives in the code and in note 01; CLAUDE.md keeps only what an AI must do. Local — your machine Concept Gotcha Best practice Paths What we covered A two-phase brief: Phase 1 investigates and proposes, Phase 2 edits only after \"do it\" Five suspected contradictions checked against the repo: three true, one partly, one false §5 \"The site\" split into rules (kept) and description (cut, −2,410 bytes) Block colours, layout and the sync rule merged into shorter forms The §2 tree gained build-index.mjs , .prog/ , .research/ and backlog-workflow/ Guide HTML synced; the guide PDF left un-rendered while keeping it is undecided Seven site details found that no note records Rule or description? Kind Test Example Goes in Rule An AI must do it, or never do it Rebuild the search index after a note changes CLAUDE.md Description How something looks or behaves Recently viewed slides open in 0.3 s The code and the meta notes Concept · A description in CLAUDE.md goes stale the moment the code changes. A rule only goes stale when the intent does. Full command sequence Local Phase 1: baseline and evidence (read-only) wc -c CLAUDE.md # -c: bytes. 16,933; the result must be below this wc -w CLAUDE.md # -w: words. 2,737, for the report ls site/pages/ .prog/ # where Complete Tasks really lives ls README.md site/build-index.mjs # do the files the tree should list exist? ls notes/fonts/ # which fonts are bundled: only Inter grep -n -i font notes/note-layout.css # -n: line no., -i: any case; DejaVu → monospace Contradictions checked Claim Verdict Fix a Complete Tasks in site/pages/ and in .prog/ True Only .prog/complete.html exists; stale line cut b Tree lists README, still on the backlog False README exists; the backlog was stale instead c build-index.mjs missing from the tree True Added d Private folders missing from the tree True One line for .prog/ and .research/ , one for backlog-workflow/ e All fonts seem bundled Partly Says DejaVu comes from the system Changes and their size # Change Section Bytes 1 \"The site\" cut to 7 rules; description points to note 01 §5 −2,410 2 Block table → one line of block → variable; \"used for\" lives in the CSS comments §3 −320 3 Layout table → two sentences §5 −250 4 Sync rule kept once, in §7 §6, §7 −180 5 Tree additions §2 +210 Local Phase 2: edit and verify node site/build-index.mjs # the guide HTML changed: rebuild the search index wc -c CLAUDE.md # 14,028: below the 16,933 baseline git diff --stat # summary: 3 files, 37 insertions, 82 deletions .claude/skills/memory-dump/check.sh # must pass Best practice · Draft the new wording in a scratch file and measure it with wc -c before proposing. Byte estimates then come from real text, not guesses. Site details no note records yet Menu button of three chalk sticks; the search tile's diagonal stick File buttons are 20:3; the search field is an inverted file button Recently viewed is the menu's mirror image, newest first Roadmap progress line, with planned modules outlined Complete Tasks' look, and old items keeping C12 , C13 , C16 Export is a small dark menu; Brave or Chrome match render.sh ; mail is a possible option The top-bar name is plain text, not a link Bugs hit & fixed Bug · grep with a context window failed CAUSE grep -o \".\\{0,90\\}word.\\{0,90\\}\" runs as ugrep here. On UTF-8 text it stops with ugrep: error: error at position 83 … exceeds complexity limits . FIX Strip the tags in Python and slice the text around each match instead. python3 -c '...re.sub(r\"<[^>]+>\", \" \", s)...' # tags out, then slice around each hit Bug · The pasted brief arrived cut short CAUSE The paste ended at step 8, so the constraints never arrived. One of them said: if a fix makes the file grow, propose shorter wording. Change 5 added 210 bytes without that offer. FIX Spotted from a screenshot of the full brief and reported against each constraint. Nothing else was broken: chalk-pastel.css , other notes and archive/ stayed untouched. Bug · Stale git step in the backlog CAUSE The backlog's \"how to work this list\" still said \"One commit per item … Push only when the user says so\", after the git rules changed. FIX It now says never commit .prog/ , and any other git step follows the user's own preferences. Where things live What Path The rules CLAUDE.md The guide, kept in sync notes/00-chalk-book-guide.html · 00-chalk-book-guide.pdf How the site looks and behaves site/ · notes/meta-01-site-build.html Drafts for measuring the session's scratchpad, never the repo Best practices Investigate, then edit A read-only phase with a proposal table lets the user cut changes before any file moves. Measure the baseline A hard size limit needs a number recorded before the first edit. Verify every claim Check suspected contradictions against the repo; one turned out false. Ask before costly steps Re-rendering the guide PDF waits while dropping it is undecided. Part of Chalk Book · follows 03 README & Git Rules · site details in 01 Site & Workflow Build",
 "pokedex-01-git-manual-push": "Pokédex · 01 · Git · 2026-10-11 Git: Manual Commit & Push From working folder to GitHub, by hand Committed today's work in the pokedex repo by hand as 44e0d5c , ready to push to GitHub alphanatic/pokeggdex over HTTPS. Along the way, learned how git status reads the three places a file can be, how to shape commit messages and trailers, and what .git/index actually contains. Local — your machine Concept Gotcha Best practice Paths What we covered The three places a file can be — working folder, index, HEAD — plus origin on GitHub A push routine: look first, then stage, commit and push Reading every section of git status , and the Starship prompt symbols Commit message shape, extra -m paragraphs, and trailers Inspected .git/index byte by byte with xxd Fixed tooling errors: missing xxd , unsynced pacman file lists, mirror 404s The big picture Concept Three places a file can be Working folder your files on disk Index (staging) .git/index — the next commit HEAD your last commit origin GitHub's copy Concept · git add moves folder → index · git commit seals index → HEAD · git push sends HEAD → origin. git status compares them: folder ≠ index = red “not staged”, index ≠ HEAD = green “to be committed”. The push routine Every push Look before you touch cd ~/Projects/pokedex # go to the repo git status # what changed, what's staged git remote -v # which GitHub repo this folder talks to git log --oneline -5 # last 5 commits, one line each New repo only Brand-new repo git init -b main # start git remote add origin <url> # link GitHub git push -u origin main # 1st push Check .gitignore first: node_modules/ , dist/ . Existing repo Your case today git pull --rebase # sync first git push # then push git remote -v already showed origin → skip the new-repo steps. Every push Stage, commit, push git add -A # stage everything: modified, deleted, new git status # check: all green git commit -m \"Title\" -m \"Description.\" # 1st -m = title, 2nd = body git push # HTTPS remote: username + access token git status # expect \"up to date with 'origin/main'\" Reading git status Section Colour Meaning Move it with On branch / up to date — Where you stand vs GitHub git pull / push Changes to be committed green Staged, goes in next commit git restore --staged Changes not staged red Tracked, edited, left out git add Untracked files red Git has never seen them git add / .gitignore Starship prompt shorthand: » renamed · ! modified · ? untracked · ✘ deleted · + staged · ↑ ahead of GitHub (push me). Commit messages Shape of a good message · Title: one short line, sentence case, what changed, no full stop. Shown by git log --oneline . Body: after a blank line — the what and why. Each extra -m = one more paragraph (no limit). One change per commit is best practice; git add -A for “everything at once” is fine when it all belongs together. git commit -m \"Lore page board, cabin and Pokédex updates\" -m \"Lore content now sits on a padded board, …\" # today's commit Concept Trailers: Key: value lines in the last paragraph Trailer Meaning Co-Authored-By: Someone helped — GitHub shows both avatars Signed-off-by: “I wrote it and may submit it” (Linux kernel style) Reviewed-by / Tested-by Credit checkers and testers Closes: #12 Auto-closes GitHub issue 12 git commit -s -m \"Title\" # -s adds Signed-off-by git commit -m \"Title\" --trailer \"Closes: #12\" # any trailer, no extra -m git log -1 --format='%(trailers)' # show only trailers Inside .git/index (with xxd) Concept · The staging area is a real binary file: a list of path → content hash, plus each file's stat info (size, timestamps, inode) so git status can skip re-reading unchanged files. File contents live in .git/objects , not here. xxd .git/index | head -8 # first 128 bytes as hex git ls-files --stage .gitignore # Git's decoded view of one entry git show :src/pokedex/pokedex.css # \":\" = the staged version of a file Offset Bytes (your repo) Meaning 0x00 4449 5243 “DIRC” — I'm a Git index 0x04 0000 0002 Format version 2 0x08 0000 0059 89 tracked files 0x0c 6ac8 e401 … Entry 1 stat data: times, inode, mode 100644, uid 1000, size 18 0x34 f062 35c4 … 1b08 Content hash (SHA-1) — the actual snapshot 0x48 000a Path is 10 characters 0x4a 2e67 6974 … “.gitignore” (first: entries sorted, “.” before letters) 0x54 0000 … 0000 1–8 NUL bytes, pads entry to a multiple of 8 Reading an xxd row: left = byte position · middle = bytes in hex · right = same bytes as text (dots = unprintable). Bugs hit & fixed Bug · git rmove -v CAUSE A typo. Git suggests the closest real command ( remote ). FIX Run the suggested git remote -v . Bug · xxd: command not found CAUSE xxd wasn't installed. zsh's helper searched pacman's file database: three packages provide it. FIX Picked tinyxxd (smallest). The default choice (1) was gvim — don't just hit Enter. Bug · database file for 'chaotic-aur' does not exist CAUSE pacman's file lists had never been synced. FIX Sync them: sudo pacman -Fy # -F: file database, -y: refresh it Bug · red 404s while installing CAUSE The mirrors had moved to a newer version than the local package list. FIX Do a full upgrade — never -Sy alone (partial upgrade): sudo pacman -Syu # -S: sync, -y: refresh lists, -u: upgrade everything Gotcha · git commit -am skips untracked files — it would have missed the new pokedex.css . Use git add -A . Unstaging a rename splits it into deleted + untracked ; re-adding both rejoins it as renamed (98%) . HTTPS remote: GitHub wants a personal access token , not your password. Open items git push # send 44e0d5c (prompt shows ↑ until done) pass insert github/token-pokeggdex # keep the token in pass git remote set-url origin git@github.com:alphanatic/pokeggdex.git # later: switch to SSH Part of the Pokédex project",
 "tinker-town-01-aws-ssh-firewall": "Project Tinker Town · 01 · Module 1 · 2026-08-30 AWS, SSH & Firewall Foundations & hardening Spun up a free-tier Ubuntu EC2 instance in eu-north-1, locked SSH down to key-only access from a single IP, and enabled UFW as a basic firewall. This is the foundation the rest of the lab connects to — every later day assumes this instance, this key, and this hardened SSH setup are already in place. Local — your machine Remote — the EC2 box Config file Gotcha Best practice Paths What we covered Launched Ubuntu Server 24.04 LTS, x86_64, t2.micro / t3.micro (free tier) Security group restricting SSH (port 22) to My IP only — not 0.0.0.0/0 Generated an ED25519 key pair — faster, smaller and more modern than RSA Moved the key to ~/.ssh/ and renamed it to match the naming convention Set up a local ~/.ssh/config so ssh lab-ubuntu-01 connects with zero extra flags Hardened sshd_config : disabled root login and password auth Installed and enabled UFW, allowing only OpenSSH Naming note · Day 1 resources use the old scheme ( aws-lab-ubuntu-01 ). Under the current rule <type>-<workload>-<env>-<region>-<instance> the key would be kp-ubuntu-lab-eun1-001 and the instance vm-ubuntu-lab-eun1-001 . Full command sequence Local Key file setup — move into ~/.ssh and rename mkdir -p ~/Projects/aws-lab # -p: no error if it exists mv ~/Documents/aws/ubuntu-lab-key.pem ~/.ssh/ # move key into ~/.ssh chmod 400 ~/.ssh/ubuntu-lab-key.pem # 400: owner read-only (required by SSH) mv ~/.ssh/ubuntu-lab-key.pem ~/.ssh/aws-lab-ubuntu-01.pem # rename to naming convention Local First connection — before the alias existed ssh -i ~/.ssh/aws-lab-ubuntu-01.pem ubuntu@<instance-ip> # -i: which private key to use Local ~/.ssh/config — connect without flags nano ~/.ssh/config # open (or create) the client config ~/.ssh/config Host lab-ubuntu-01 alias you type after ssh HostName <instance-ip> real address (changes on stop/start) User adm-kiwi personal admin login IdentityFile ~/.ssh/aws-lab-ubuntu-01.pem key used automatically Gotcha · SSH config files don't support comments at the end of a line — a # only works at the start of its own line. That's why the notes sit beside the config here instead of inside it. chmod 600 ~/.ssh/config # 600: owner read/write only ssh lab-ubuntu-01 # connect using the alias Remote Fix terminal rendering (Kitty terminfo) export TERM=xterm-256color # fixes \"Error opening terminal: xterm-kitty\" Remote Harden sshd_config sudo nano /etc/ssh/sshd_config # edit the SSH server config as root /etc/ssh/sshd_config PermitRootLogin no nobody logs in directly as root PasswordAuthentication no keys only, no password guessing PubkeyAuthentication yes allow key-based login sudo sshd -t # -t: test config syntax BEFORE restarting sudo systemctl restart ssh # apply the changes Remote UFW firewall sudo ufw allow OpenSSH # allow SSH before enabling, avoids lockout sudo ufw enable # turn the firewall on sudo ufw status # confirm the active rules Remote Close the session exit # or Ctrl+D Where things live What Path Private key (local) ~/.ssh/aws-lab-ubuntu-01.pem Local SSH config ~/.ssh/config SSH daemon config (remote) /etc/ssh/sshd_config SSH config overrides (remote) /etc/ssh/sshd_config.d/ Original key download (moved out of) ~/Documents/aws/ Best practices Scope SSH to a known IP Never leave port 22 open to 0.0.0.0/0 . ED25519 over RSA Smaller, faster, and no known weaknesses for new keys. Uncommenting ≠ changing Removing # keeps the old value — edit the value itself. Test before restart Run sshd -t before restarting anything that could lock you out. Keep a fallback session Leave one SSH session open while testing a config change. Public IPs move A stopped/started instance gets a new IP unless an Elastic IP is attached. Keys live in ~/.ssh/ Not in a project folder — that's where SSH tooling expects them. Lock down permissions 400 on keys, 600 on config — SSH rejects looser ones. Part of Project Tinker Town · see 05 Naming Conventions for the naming reference",
 "tinker-town-02-linux-users-accounts": "Project Tinker Town · 02 · Module 1 · 2026-08-30 Linux Users & Accounts Admin users & service accounts Created a dedicated sudo admin account ( adm-kiwi ) instead of using the default ubuntu user, then built a proper no-login service account ( svc-disk-report ) to run background scripts under least privilege. This establishes the identity model the rest of the lab's services build on. Remote — the server Concept Gotcha Best practice Paths What we covered Created adm-kiwi as a personal sudo admin account Username rules — dots ( . ) are invalid; landed on hyphenated naming Copied the SSH key to the new user so it could authenticate directly Explored /etc/passwd and /etc/group — how Linux actually stores user and group data Learned the “default private group” mechanism behind adduser Personal admin accounts vs. dedicated service accounts — least privilege, blast radius, auditability Created svc-disk-report as a no-login service account Full command sequence Remote Create the personal admin account sudo adduser adm-kiwi # create user + home directory sudo usermod -aG sudo adm-kiwi # -aG: append to the sudo group (keeps others) groups adm-kiwi # verify group membership Remote Copy the SSH key to the new user sudo mkdir -p /home/adm-kiwi/.ssh # -p: no error if it exists sudo cp ~/.ssh/authorized_keys /home/adm-kiwi/.ssh/authorized_keys # copy the allowed public key sudo chown -R adm-kiwi:adm-kiwi /home/adm-kiwi/.ssh # -R: hand folder to the user sudo chmod 700 /home/adm-kiwi/.ssh # 700: only owner can enter sudo chmod 600 /home/adm-kiwi/.ssh/authorized_keys # 600: owner read/write only Gotcha · SSH silently refuses keys if the .ssh folder or authorized_keys is owned by root or too open — the chown and chmod steps are what make the login work. Remote Delete a user (if starting over) sudo deluser --remove-home kiwi # --remove-home: delete user + home dir Remote Inspect users & groups grep adm-kiwi /etc/passwd # user's UID / primary GID / shell grep adm-kiwi /etc/group # group memberships id adm-kiwi # numeric UID/GID + all groups, human-readable Tidier form · The original used cat /etc/passwd | grep adm-kiwi . grep can read the file itself, so the cat and pipe aren't needed — same result. Remote Create a service account sudo useradd -r -s /usr/sbin/nologin svc-disk-report # -r: system acct, -s: no-login shell How Linux stores accounts Concept /etc/passwd format username:x:UID:GID:comment:homedir:shell The 4th field (GID) points to the user's primary group — the one auto-created by adduser with the same name as the user. Concept /etc/group format groupname:x:GID:member1,member2,... The member list only holds additional members (added via usermod -aG ) — not the user whose primary group this is. Where things live What Path User accounts /etc/passwd Group definitions /etc/group New user's authorized keys /home/<user>/.ssh/authorized_keys Service account shell (blocks login) /usr/sbin/nologin Best practices Leave the default user behind Don't work as the provider's default user ( ubuntu ) long-term — create your own admin account. Humans and services apart Personal sudo accounts are for you; background services get their own dedicated accounts. Service accounts are locked down No login shell, no sudo, no password — least privilege by design. One account per service Contains the blast radius and gives a clearer audit trail. Follow the naming rule adm-kiwi for personal admin, svc-<purpose> for services. Build, then hand over Build and test as your own account first, then give ownership to the service account once it works. Part of Project Tinker Town · see 05 Naming Conventions for the full naming reference",
 "tinker-town-03-systemd-service": "Project Tinker Town · 03 · Module 1 · 2026-08-30 Systemd Service Building a custom background service Built hello-loop.service — a small custom systemd unit running a self-trimming logger script, from scratch. Along the way, hit and fixed three real bugs (broken shebang, log permissions, restart rate-limiting) — exactly the kind of troubleshooting loop real systemd work involves. Remote — the server Config file Gotcha Best practice Paths What we covered Wrote hello-loop.sh — a script logging a timestamp on an interval Placed it in /usr/local/bin/ — the convention for sysadmin-authored scripts Built a systemd unit file with [Unit] / [Service] / [Install] sections Learned the daemon-reload → enable → start → status workflow Debugged a broken shebang line, a log permissions error, and a restart rate-limit lockout Refactored the log to self-trim to the last 5 lines, in memory, with no temp file Full command sequence Remote Create the script sudo nano /usr/local/bin/hello-loop.sh # create the script as root /usr/local/bin/hello-loop.sh #!/bin/bash run with bash while true; do loop forever echo \"Hello from systemd at $(date)\" >> /var/log/hello-loop.log >>: append a line echo \"$(tail -n 5 /var/log/hello-loop.log)\" > /var/log/hello-loop.log keep the last 5 lines sleep 60 wait 60 seconds done sudo chmod +x /usr/local/bin/hello-loop.sh # +x: make it executable Remote Create the systemd unit file sudo nano /etc/systemd/system/hello-loop.service # create the unit file /etc/systemd/system/hello-loop.service [Unit] metadata + ordering Description=Hello Loop Test Service shown in systemctl status After=network.target start after networking is up [Service] how to run it ExecStart=/usr/local/bin/hello-loop.sh the command to run Restart=always restart it whenever it stops User=adm-kiwi run as this user, not root [Install] how it hooks into boot WantedBy=multi-user.target start in the normal boot state Gotcha · Unit files only allow comments on their own line. Text after a value is read as part of the value, so the notes sit beside the file here instead of inside it. Remote Enable, start, verify sudo systemctl daemon-reload # pick up the new unit file sudo systemctl enable hello-loop.service # start on boot sudo systemctl start hello-loop.service # start now sudo systemctl status hello-loop.service # check it's running cat /var/log/hello-loop.log # confirm it's writing Bugs hit & fixed Bug · broken shebang from an inline comment CAUSE #!/bin/bash # comment — the kernel treats the whole shebang line as one interpreter path plus argument, so it tried to run bash with # comment and failed with exit code 127 . FIX Comments go on their own line — never on the shebang line itself. Bug · log permission denied CAUSE The service ran as adm-kiwi , but /var/log/ is root-owned — the user couldn't create files there. FIX Create the log file as root, then hand that one file to the service user: sudo touch /var/log/hello-loop.log # create the empty log file sudo chown adm-kiwi:adm-kiwi /var/log/hello-loop.log # give it to the service user Bug · restart rate-limit lockout CAUSE After several failed starts, systemd refused further attempts (“Start request repeated too quickly”). FIX Clear the failed state, then start again: sudo systemctl reset-failed hello-loop.service # clear the crash counter sudo systemctl start hello-loop.service # try again Where things live What Path Custom scripts (sysadmin-authored) /usr/local/bin/ Distro-managed binaries /usr/bin/ Systemd unit files /etc/systemd/system/ hello-loop's log /var/log/hello-loop.log Best practices No comments on the shebang The kernel reads the whole line as the interpreter path. Own the log file up front Give a service's log file explicit ownership before the service ever runs, not after it fails. Unstick a crash loop Run systemctl reset-failed when a crash-looped service refuses to restart. Trim in memory Prefer echo \"$(tail ...)\" > file over a temp-file-and-move dance when the directory isn't writable by the service user. Always daemon-reload Run it after creating or editing any unit file. Scripts in /usr/local/bin Keep your own scripts apart from distro binaries in /usr/bin/ . Part of Project Tinker Town · see 05 Naming Conventions for the full naming reference",
 "tinker-town-04-cron-logrotate": "Project Tinker Town · 04 · Module 1 · 2026-08-30 Cron & Logrotate Scheduled jobs & log rotation Built a scheduled disk-usage report ( disk-report.sh ) via cron, then set up logrotate so its log never grows unbounded. Along the way, migrated the whole pipeline to a dedicated svc-disk-report service account — verified end-to-end with a real forced rotation. Remote — the server Config file Concept Gotcha Best practice Paths What we covered Wrote disk-report.sh — timestamp + df -h output Scheduled it with crontab -e — 5-field time syntax, per-user schedules Why unbounded logs are a real problem, and how logrotate solves it Built a /etc/logrotate.d/ config: daily rotation, compression, 7-day retention Debugged real logrotate errors (inline comments, insecure directory permissions) Migrated the entire pipeline — script, log folder, cron entry, logrotate su — to run as svc-disk-report Verified with a forced rotation: disk-report.log.1.gz was created Full command sequence Remote The script sudo nano /usr/local/bin/disk-report.sh # create the script as root /usr/local/bin/disk-report.sh #!/bin/bash run with bash echo \"Disk report at $(date)\" >> /var/log/disk-report/disk-report.log timestamp header df -h >> /var/log/disk-report/disk-report.log -h: human-readable sizes echo \"---\" >> /var/log/disk-report/disk-report.log separator line sudo chmod +x /usr/local/bin/disk-report.sh # +x: make it executable Concept Cron's 5-field syntax * * * * * command │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ └─ day of week (0-7, 0 and 7 = Sunday) │ │ │ └────── month (1-12) │ │ └─────────── day of month (1-31) │ └──────────────── hour (0-23) └───────────────────── minute (0-59) Remote Schedule it crontab -e # -e: edit your own crontab crontab entry 0 * * * * /usr/local/bin/disk-report.sh minute 0 of every hour = hourly, on the hour crontab -l # -l: list what's scheduled Remote Logrotate config sudo nano /etc/logrotate.d/disk-report # create the rotation rule /etc/logrotate.d/disk-report /var/log/disk-report/disk-report.log { which log this rule covers su svc-disk-report svc-disk-report rotate as this user + group daily rotate once a day rotate 7 keep 7 old copies compress gzip old copies missingok no error if the log is missing notifempty skip rotation if it's empty copytruncate copy, then empty the live file } Bugs hit & fixed Bug · inline comments broke the config CAUSE Unlike bash, logrotate doesn't treat # as “ignore the rest of the line” — rotate 7 # comment was read as one invalid value. FIX Comments go on their own line, never after a setting. Bug · insecure directory permissions CAUSE logrotate (running as root) refused to touch a log whose parent directory wasn't strictly root-owned. FIX Added the su <user> <group> directive so logrotate rotates as the account that owns the log. Bug · file owned, but not the directory CAUSE Rotation creates a new file ( disk-report.log.1 ) in the parent directory — owning only the log file wasn't enough. FIX Gave the log its own dedicated subfolder, owned entirely by the service account. Remote Migrate to svc-disk-report cd /var/log # work in /var/log sudo mkdir -p disk-report # -p: no error if it exists sudo chown svc-disk-report:svc-disk-report disk-report # hand folder to the svc sudo mv disk-report.log disk-report/disk-report.log # move log into its folder sudo chown svc-disk-report:svc-disk-report disk-report/disk-report.log # hand log to the svc sudo crontab -u svc-disk-report -e # -u: the svc's crontab Remote Test the full pipeline sudo logrotate -d /etc/logrotate.d/disk-report # -d: dry run, checks syntax only sudo logrotate -f /etc/logrotate.d/disk-report # -f: force a real rotation now ls -la /var/log/disk-report/ # -la: list all, long format; look for .log.1.gz Where things live What Path Report script /usr/local/bin/disk-report.sh Log directory (own folder per service) /var/log/disk-report/ Logrotate config /etc/logrotate.d/disk-report Personal crontab crontab -e / crontab -l Another user's crontab sudo crontab -u <user> -e Best practices Plan rotation up front Any log that grows unattended needs a rotation policy — decide it before it's a problem. One log folder per service Matches real-world convention ( nginx , postgres and others). Check comment rules per tool Config files aren't always inline-comment-safe like bash — read each tool's syntax rules. Dry-run first Always logrotate -d before forcing a real rotation. Jobs run as their service Background jobs belong to their dedicated service account, not your personal sudo account. Use full paths in cron Cron runs with a minimal environment, so absolute paths avoid “command not found”. Part of Project Tinker Town · see 05 Naming Conventions for the full naming reference",
 "tinker-town-05-naming-conventions": "Project Tinker Town · 05 · Module 1 · 2026-08-30 Naming Conventions Cross-cutting reference Every naming pattern established across the lab so far — AWS resources, admin vs. service accounts, systemd units, and log/config files. This file is a reference, not a walkthrough — check back here whenever you name something new in later days. Best practice Paths Core philosophy Every name should tell you, at a glance, what it's for and what category it belongs to : type / workload / env / region fields for cloud resources adm- / svc- prefixes instantly separate a human's admin account from a service's unattended account Consistent 1:1 naming between a service and its supporting files (unit file, log folder, logrotate config) so nothing requires memory to trace Updated rule · Cloud naming moved to the professional pattern <type>-<workload>-<env>-<region>-<instance> , the widely used Microsoft Cloud Adoption Framework style. Patterns 1 and 2 show the old form crossed out. Day 1 resources still carry the old names, e.g. aws-lab-ubuntu-01 . The patterns AWS resources (key pairs, VPCs, subnets, security groups…) <type>-<workload>-<env>-<region>-<instance> Type first so resources group in lists; short region codes. Example: kp-ubuntu-lab-eun1-001 Replaces aws-<env>-<purpose>-<id> EC2 instances vm-<workload>-<env>-<region>-<instance> Same rule with type vm . Example: vm-ubuntu-lab-eun1-001 Replaces <env>-<purpose>-<id> Personal sudo/admin username adm-kiwi Dots are invalid in Linux usernames — only letters, digits, _ and - . Service accounts svc-<purpose> No login shell, no sudo. Example: svc-disk-report Systemd unit files <purpose>.service No extra prefix — location + extension already say what it is. Example: hello-loop.service Log directories /var/log/<service-name>/<service-name>.log One dedicated folder per service. Example: /var/log/disk-report/disk-report.log Logrotate config files /etc/logrotate.d/<service-name> No extension — matches the .d folder convention. Example: /etc/logrotate.d/disk-report Quick reference What Pattern Example AWS resource <type>-<workload>-<env>-<region>-<instance> kp-ubuntu-lab-eun1-001 EC2 instance vm-<workload>-<env>-<region>-<instance> vm-ubuntu-lab-eun1-001 Personal admin user adm-kiwi adm-kiwi Service account svc-<purpose> svc-disk-report Systemd unit <purpose>.service hello-loop.service Log directory /var/log/<service-name>/ /var/log/disk-report/ Logrotate config /etc/logrotate.d/<service-name> /etc/logrotate.d/disk-report Common type prefixes Type Prefix Type Prefix EC2 instance vm Security group sg Key pair kp Subnet snet VPC vpc Route table rt Internet gateway igw NAT gateway nat Part of Project Tinker Town · indexes 01 AWS, SSH & Firewall , 02 Linux Users & Accounts , 03 Systemd Service and 04 Cron & Logrotate",
 "tinker-town-06-storage-filesystems": "Project Tinker Town · 06 · Module 2 · 2026-08-30 Storage & Filesystems EBS volumes & LVM Attached a second EBS volume, formatted it, mounted it permanently via /etc/fstab , and grew it live from 5 GB to 6 GB. Then built a full LVM stack from scratch on a third volume — Physical Volume, Volume Group, Logical Volume — grew it live, and cleanly tore it all down afterward. Remote — the server Config file Concept AWS Console Gotcha Best practice Paths What we covered Created and attached a second EBS volume, matching the instance's Availability Zone AWS device names ( /dev/sdd ) vs. the actual Linux/NVMe names ( /dev/nvme1n1 ) Formatted with ext4, mounted manually, then made it permanent via /etc/fstab using the UUID Verified the fstab entry safely with umount + mount -a before trusting a reboot Grew the volume in the AWS Console (5 GB → 6 GB) and live-resized the filesystem to match Built a full LVM stack on a third volume: PV → VG → LV Hit and resolved a real extent-rounding error when extending an LV Live-extended the LV using all remaining free space in the pool Cleanly tore down the LVM stack and removed the test volume Part 1 — EBS volume, ext4 & fstab AWS Console Create & attach the volume Key rule · The volume's Availability Zone must exactly match the instance's AZ (e.g. eu-north-1b ) — otherwise it can't be attached. Remote Identify the new disk lsblk # list all block devices nvme1n1 259:5 0 5G 0 disk # the new, empty volume — AWS called it /dev/sdd Remote Format & mount sudo mkfs.ext4 /dev/nvme1n1 # create an ext4 filesystem on the raw disk sudo mkdir -p /mnt/data # -p: create the mountpoint sudo mount /dev/nvme1n1 /mnt/data # mount it df -h /mnt/data # -h: confirm it's mounted, human sizes Remote Make the mount permanent sudo blkid /dev/nvme1n1 # get the filesystem's UUID sudo nano /etc/fstab # add the mount line /etc/fstab UUID=f707c3d8-b4d8-4932-b350-4776695c932d /mnt/data ext4 defaults 0 2 UUID=… /mnt/data ext4 defaults 0 2 which filesystem where to mount filesystem type standard options dump: skip backup fsck order (after root) Gotcha · fstab only documents comments as lines that start with # — keep notes on their own line, never after the fields. The field table above does the explaining instead. sudo umount /mnt/data # unmount (note: \"umount\", no \"n\") sudo mount -a # -a: mount all fstab entries, like a boot df -h /mnt/data # confirm it came back via fstab Concept Why UUID, not device name Concept · Device names like /dev/nvme1n1 can shift when disks are added or removed. A filesystem's UUID is permanent and unique, so fstab always finds the right disk regardless of the order disks are detected in. Remote Live resize after growing the volume in AWS lsblk # confirm the kernel sees the new size (6G) sudo resize2fs /dev/nvme1n1 # grow the filesystem to fill the disk df -h /mnt/data # confirm ~6G, no unmount needed Part 2 — Full LVM stack Concept The LVM stack Disk → Physical Volume (PV) → Volume Group (VG) → Logical Volume (LV) → Filesystem → Mount Remote Build PV → VG → LV on a third volume sudo pvcreate /dev/nvme2n1 # mark the disk as LVM-managed sudo vgcreate vg-data /dev/nvme2n1 # create a pool from the PV sudo lvcreate -L 3G -n lv-data vg-data # -L: size, -n: name of the new LV Remote Format & mount the LV sudo mkfs.ext4 /dev/vg-data/lv-data # ext4 filesystem on the LV sudo mkdir -p /mnt/lvm-data # -p: create the mountpoint sudo mount /dev/vg-data/lv-data /mnt/lvm-data # mount it df -h /mnt/lvm-data # confirm size and mount Bug · extent rounding on lvextend CAUSE lvextend -L +1G failed — “256 extents needed, but only 255 available.” VG metadata takes a few MiB, so the pool had slightly less than a clean 1 GiB free. FIX Use -l +100%FREE to take all remaining space instead of an exact size. Remote Live-extend the LV sudo lvextend -l +100%FREE /dev/vg-data/lv-data # -l: grow by all free space in the pool sudo resize2fs /dev/vg-data/lv-data # grow the filesystem to match df -h /mnt/lvm-data # confirm ~3.9G, still mounted throughout Remote Tear down the LVM stack sudo umount /mnt/lvm-data # unmount first sudo lvremove /dev/vg-data/lv-data # remove the LV (confirm with y) sudo vgremove vg-data # remove the volume group sudo pvremove /dev/nvme2n1 # release the disk from LVM sudo rmdir /mnt/lvm-data # remove the empty mountpoint AWS Console Remove the test volume Cleanup · Detach the 4 GiB LVM test volume once it shows Available , then delete it. The 6 GiB /mnt/data volume stays — it's real, mounted infrastructure, not a test. Where things live What Path Permanent mount config /etc/fstab Direct-disk mountpoint /mnt/data LVM logical volume path /dev/<vg-name>/<lv-name> LVM device-mapper path /dev/mapper/<vg>-<lv> Best practices Match the AZ The EBS volume's AZ must match the instance's AZ before attaching. UUID in fstab Not device names — those can shift. Test before rebooting Check a new fstab entry with umount + mount -a before trusting a real reboot. Grow in two steps An EBS volume grows live in AWS, but the filesystem must be resized separately to see it. LVM resizes live Resize storage without unmounting — a real advantage over a raw disk. Use +100%FREE Prefer -l +100%FREE over an exact -L size to avoid extent-rounding failures. Tear down in order LV → VG → PV, before removing the underlying disk. Name LVM parts clearly Prefixes like vg- and lv- make the stack readable at a glance. Part of Project Tinker Town · see 05 Naming Conventions for the naming reference"
};
